<!-- PBRVoxel for the faces the mesher put on the chunk's "alpha" child:
     water, and anything else a game gave a real alpha to. The same shader as
     PBRVoxel.xml, blended instead of cut out, and with culling off so that a
     water surface is still there when the camera is under it.

     The blended pass is the ambient one and litalpha is the sun on top of
     it, which is what puts a highlight on water. Urho3D runs the pair the
     way it runs base and light on an opaque surface, so the light is added
     once rather than the surface being drawn twice -- which matters here,
     because a blended quad drawn twice blends twice and reads as opaque.

     No depth or shadow pass, so water and glass cast none. That is the right
     default: a transparent thing casting an opaque shadow looks worse than
     it casting none, and it does mean a glass roof lets full sun through.

     simplified: no VOXELMODIFIERS variant, so a world that binds the voxel
     format's surface modifiers loses them on its translucent voxels. Nothing
     binds both yet; the upgrade path is the same copy PBRVoxelModifiers.xml
     is of PBRVoxel.xml. -->
<technique vs="voxel_shading/PBRVoxel" ps="voxel_shading/PBRVoxel"
        psdefines="DIFFMAP PBR METALLIC VOXELNORMALMAP VOXELIBL VOXELSPOTS VOXELTRANSLUCENCY">
    <pass name="alpha" blend="alpha" depthwrite="false" cull="none" />
    <pass name="litalpha" blend="addalpha" depthwrite="false" cull="none" />
</technique>
